WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.339
The ending of the horror-infused Jurassic
World 2 ushers in a new Jurassic reality.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:10.820
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm breaking down what Fallen Kingdom's

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.630
ending and post-credits scene really mean
and how they set up Jurassic World 3, and

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:20.080
I'll also be explaining some clever foreshadowing
of key plot points and easter eggs in the

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.080
movie.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.300
Spoilers ahead for Jurassic World 2, so take
care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.829
"Welcome to Jurassic World."

00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:32.640
Yes, it's Dr Malcolm who bookends the movie,
delivering the last spoken line and confirming

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.942
that we're no longer going to be watching
dinosaurs rampaging on an island.

00:00:35.942 --> 00:00:41.930
Isla Nublar was destroyed earlier in the film,
and against Dr Malcolm's recommendation, a

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.170
selection of the prehistoric creatures were
rescued and have now ended up loose on the

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.170
mainland.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.750
As Malcolm says, both humans and dinos will
have to figure out how to co-exist.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.870
It's an Empire Strikes Back-style cliffhanger
ending that – coupled with the reveal that

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.000
Benjamin Lockwood has successfully used human
cloning to create Maisie from the DNA of his

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.830
dead daughter – has far-reaching implications
for what we can expect in Jurassic World 3

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:05.940
and beyond.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.640
Blue and T-Rex are the two dinos given the
money shots in the final scene, which I'm

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.690
sure means we'll see them return for key roles
in the sequel.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.150
Blue refused to go back into captivity with
Owen and we see him looking out over a town

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.150
at the end.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.940
Henry Wu, who survived the sequel carnage
for a second time, didn't get the blood sample

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.770
from the raptor that he was looking for but
I think there could be groups trying to track

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.920
the valuable raptor down in the next film.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.040
T-Rex had that final shot breaking into a
lion enclosure at a zoo and that roar might

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.630
suggest she's come to take over as the new
queen of the jungle.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:42.540
Oh and I'm sure the Mosasaurus, who we see
among surfers in the ocean, will also return

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.080
for more of the chomp shots and Jaws easter
eggs we've seen so far in the Jurassic World

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.080
movies.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.020
There was also a post-credits scene which
revealed where the Pteranodons end up.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.260
Owen and Claire had seen them fly along the
coastline after they escaped, but the end-credits

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.640
scene reveals that the flying reptiles have
made it to Las Vegas.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.970
The shot of them nesting in the fake Eiffel
Tower in Vegas is a bit of commentary about

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:09.119
clones taking over from their original counterparts,
and you could even see it as some meta-commentary

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:11.239
about the new films versus the originals.

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:16.040
Plus it also shows that the dinos are making
their way inland with the potential danger

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.810
that they could overrun the North American
mainland in the next sequel if they manage

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.810
to reproduce.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.159
As Dr Malcolm would say, "Life finds a way".

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:28.999
Talking of Ian Malcolm, during his final monologue,
he also mentions that genetic power has been

00:02:28.999 --> 00:02:33.279
unleashed which, as well as the more obvious
reference to the resurrected dinosaurs that

00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:37.700
are now roaming the land, is also a reference
to the revelation in Fallen Kingdom that Benjamin

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.189
Lockwood took the genetic cloning technology
he developed with John Hammond and used it

00:02:42.189 --> 00:02:47.569
on his dead daughter to create a new clone
of her: Maisie, who he calls his granddaughter.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:50.810
Maisie's cloned nature was foreshadowed numerous
times in the movie.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.430
There was the scene at the beginning in the
Lockwood Estate where Maisie frightens the

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:59.239
housekeeper Iris by darting around the dinosaur
exhibit room making her think there's a wild

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:00.609
animal loose.

00:03:00.609 --> 00:03:04.930
Iris also lectured Maisie on how she should
pronounce the word "bath", telling the young

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.609
girl that she's not a wild animal.

00:03:07.609 --> 00:03:11.430
And Lockwood himself told Maisie that her
mother would have saved all the dinosaurs

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.459
on Isla Nublar, which is exactly what Maisie
later did at the end of the movie when she

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:17.249
opened the doors to free them.

00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:21.519
Of course, the photo Lockwood kept hidden
was a big clue as it showed a younger Iris

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:24.870
together with Lockwood's daughter who looked
identical to Maisie.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.340
But even before that picture was revealed,
we'd already heard Lockwood tell Maisie that

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.159
she looked so much like her mother that "[she]
could be her mirror image".

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:37.480
Jeff Goldblum's return to the franchise in
Fallen Kingdom had a lot of fans excited and

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.579
although he basically only had a kind of glorified
cameo, Dr Malcolm's opinions and commentary

00:03:42.579 --> 00:03:46.370
on the subject of "animals being exploited
for entertainment or money-making" are, of

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.769
course, pertinent to both the Jurassic World
movies so far.

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:54.669
As a nice easter egg, screenwriter Colin Trevorrow
has also said that much of Goldblum's dialogue

00:03:54.669 --> 00:03:58.269
in Fallen Kingdom is actually taken "from
Michael Crichton's novel."

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:02.599
And given the way the film ends, I think there's
a good opportunity for Goldblum to return

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:06.099
in Jurassic World 3 in a much more significant
way.

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:10.260
Another character who we'll likely see return
for Jurassic World 3 is Henry Wu.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.109
Wu survived the fall-out from yet another
Jurassic World movie and we also saw a briefcase

00:04:15.109 --> 00:04:18.000
at the end full of samples from many dinosaur
breeds.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.690
There are also the various shady individuals,
mercenaries and arms dealers who made away

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:27.070
with several dinosaurs from the auction before
the Indoraptor was set loose, so we could

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.220
see those groups start to implement their
nefarious plans for the animals in Jurassic

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.220
World 3.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.650
Ok, so, what are some of the other possibilities
we can look forward to in the next movie?

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:41.450
Well, speaking about the trilogy, Colin Trevorrow
has said that "Jurassic World was an action

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:46.780
adventure, Fallen Kingdom is kind of a horror
suspense film, and Jurassic World 3 will be

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.030
a science thriller in the same way that Jurassic
Park was."

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.940
And I wonder whether there could be a different
type of DNA hybridisation explored.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.160
What I mean is that it's interesting how at
the very end of Fallen Kingdom, the camera

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.720
moved in for a lingering close-up of Maisie's
face, making me think she's probably also

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.680
got a significant part to play in the next
movie.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:10.190
We know she was cloned, but have no idea about
any of the details.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.900
For example, is it possible that an element
of non-human DNA such as dinosaur DNA was

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:16.900
used?

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.900
Although I thought the Indoraptor was probably
just trying to hunt her, at one point I wondered

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.630
whether there was a hint that it was semi-curious
about her, like they were tangentially connected

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.210
in some way.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.080
Human-dino hybrids have been considered for
Jurassic movies in the past but the idea never

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.100
got off the ground.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:37.390
Something that we won't be seeing in Jurassic
World 3 though is any more hybrid dinosaurs

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.040
such as the Indominus Rex or the Indoraptor.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.110
In fact, T-Rex crushed the Indominus Rex sample
from Isla Nublar under her foot as she walked

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.380
off into the woods.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:50.580
Trevorrow has said that for Jurassic World
3 he's planning to get "back into the wild

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.200
animal, true dinosaur nature of all of it."

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.610
I also want to mention a couple of very interesting
easter eggs.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:01.070
When Eli Mills dies at the end of the movie,
T-Rex and the Carnotaurus fight over him and

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:06.020
rip him apart, which is a call-back to how
Eddie Carr was killed by two T-Rexs in The

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.550
Lost World.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:12.360
And the heart-breaking Brachiosaurus scene
as the boat left Isla Nublar cleverly referenced

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.080
the first Jurassic Park movie in a special
way.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.710
In the original movie, the Brachiosaurus was
the very first dinosaur we saw and the way

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.740
it reared up on its hind legs in its dying
moments in Fallen Kingdom was just like it

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.530
did in that scene in Jurassic Park.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:30.740
Another subtle and moving detail was as the
smoke engulfed the poor creature, the burning

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:35.810
lava behind it glowed orange forming a silhouette
of the dinosaur giving it a look almost like

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.930
it was being preserved in amber.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:42.450
The mosquito set in amber is where John Hammond
got the dino DNA that he used to bring them

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:47.410
back to life in Jurassic Park, so seeing the
shadow of the Brachiosaurus set against the

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:52.090
orange-yellow colour of the lava felt like
everything was coming full circle.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:56.350
So, did you enjoy Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom
and how do you feel about the direction the

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:57.970
franchise has taken?

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.810
And what would you like to see in Jurassic
World 3?

